If I were upgrading the Wintersys Agile Deployment Toolkit (ADT) to modernize its secrets management—without losing its lightweight, POSIX-shell, multi-cloud portability—I would build a pluggable, zero-secret-in-disk architecture centered around Mozilla SOPS (with Age) for static bootstrap secrets and Infisical or HashiCorp Vault for dynamic runtime injection.
Right now, ADT relies on writing plain-text descriptor files (database_descriptor.dat, ApplicationCredentials.sh) on the build machine and passing them into cloud-init user-data. If the build machine is compromised or a cloud provider's instance metadata service is exposed, those secrets are readable.
Here is the exact architectural upgrade I would implement across ADT’s lifecycle:

1. Bootstrap Phase: Replace Plain-Text Templates with SOPS + Age

To let users keep configuration in Git safely (even in public forks or team repos) without needing a running server first, I would integrate SOPS (Secrets OPerationS) paired with Age encryption.
• How it works: Instead of storing plain-text credentials in template override scripts, users store an encrypted secrets.enc.env or secrets.enc.yaml file in their build-machine fork.
• The Only Secret Needed: The user only passes a single SOPS_AGE_KEY via cloud-init user-data when booting the build machine.
• In-Memory Decryption: Inside ADT’s build scripts (expedited or hardcore), instead of writing ApplicationCredentials.sh to disk, the script runs:sh
eval "$(sops -d --output-type dotenv secrets.enc.yaml)"
Use code with caution.
This loads all API keys (Cloudflare, DigitalOcean/Linode/Vultr, Git tokens) directly into the build shell's volatile memory (RAM) without ever touching the filesystem.

2. Provisioning Phase: Ephemeral Bootstrap Tokens Instead of Raw Secrets in Cloud-Init

Cloud-init user-data scripts are stored in the VPS provider's control plane and are often readable via the local metadata API (169.254.169.254) from inside the VM. Passing raw database passwords or S3 keys in user-data is a major risk.
• The Upgrade: Strip all actual secrets out of the webserver, database, and autoscaler cloud-init scripts.
• How it works:
	1. When the build machine spins up a new webserver node, it calls a secret manager API (such as Infisical, Doppler, or HashiCorp Vault) to generate a short-lived, single-use bootstrap token (TTL: 5–10 minutes, IP-restricted to the new droplet/instance).
	2. Only that temporary token is injected into the node's cloud-init user-data.
	3. On boot, the node exchanges the temporary token for its actual runtime secrets (DB credentials, S3 keys, SMTP passwords) over HTTPS. Once exchanged, the bootstrap token immediately self-destructs.

3. Runtime Phase: Inject Secrets into tmpfs (RAM Disk) or Environment

ADT currently writes application configurations and credentials directly to the web node's disk so PHP/web applications can read them.
• The Upgrade: Mount a small tmpfs (RAM-backed filesystem, e.g., /run/adt-secrets) with 0700 permissions owned strictly by the web server user (www-data).
• How it works: The provisioning script pulls the secrets from the secret store directly into /run/adt-secrets/env.php (or .env).
	• Because it lives in tmpfs, secrets never touch the physical SSD/NVMe, survive zero reboots if a disk snapshot is stolen, and cannot be leaked via accidental backups of /var/www.
	• Adding a lightweight systemd timer or cron hook on the web/autoscaler nodes allows zero-downtime secret rotation: when a database password changes in the vault, the nodes pull the updated file into /run/adt-secrets and reload PHP-FPM/Nginx.

4. Architectural Summary: Current ADT vs. Upgraded ADT


Lifecycle Stage	Current ADT Behavior	Upgraded ADT Architecture
Repo / Config Storage	Plain-text templates in private Git forks or local override scripts.	SOPS + Age encrypted files committed safely to Git.
Build Machine State	Writes .dat and .sh credential files to ${BUILD_HOME}.	Decrypts directly into shell environment variables in RAM; zero disk writes.
Cloud-Init / Node Boot	Passes raw DB/S3 credentials inside cloud-init user-data.	Passes a 5-minute single-use Vault/Infisical token in user-data.
Web / DB Node Runtime	Credentials written to standard disk directories.	Secrets pulled at boot into an encrypted tmpfs RAM mount (/run/adt-secrets).


Why This Fits Wintersys ADT’s Philosophy

Wintersys ADT is designed to be lean, vendor-agnostic, and free of heavy orchestration bloat like Kubernetes or Terraform. Using SOPS + Age (a single static Go binary with zero external dependencies) alongside a lightweight REST API call (curl + jq) to Infisical or Vault keeps ADT 100% compatible with POSIX shell scripts across Debian/Ubuntu while eliminating plain-text secrets from disk, Git, and cloud-init metadata.
